WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.150
I know you are wondering
what coin flipping has to do

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:05.860
with k-means clustering.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.260
I promise I will explain in this section.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.710
And in the previous section,
we learned that, if HiddenVector is known,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:18.330
we can derive Parameters
through dot products.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.020
But, in reality, neither HiddenVector
nor Parameters are known,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.300
and therefore, it is unclear how
to derive the biases of the coin.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.790
Let's start from a simple problem.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:34.840
Let's imagine for a second that
Parameters are actually known.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.880
Let's start from this example.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.278
Parameters are 0.35 for the
blue coin, 0.80 for the green coin.

00:00:42.278 --> 00:00:45.920
Can we derive HiddenVector?

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.475
Well let's say what the most likely
coin is to generate the first

00:00:51.475 --> 00:00:56.480
sequence of flips, where the
fraction of heads is 0.4.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.540
Well, we can compute the probability
that the first sequence

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.490
was generated by the blue coin,

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.220
and this probability
turns out to be 0.00113.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:12.720
We can also compute
the probability that it was

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.620
generated by the green coin, and it turns
out to be much smaller number: 0.00003.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:22.410
Which coin is more likely?

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:27.770
Of course, the blue coin is more
likely to generate the first sequence.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.750
Which coin is more likely to generate the
second sequence, with nine heads?

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:38.890
Well, we do the same calculation, and
it turns out that the probability of

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.770
the second series of flips to
be generated by the blue coin

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.610
is much smaller than the probability of
it being generated by the green coin.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:55.080
Therefore, the most likely coin for
the second series of flips is green.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.120
And by continuing this way,
we can derive HiddenVector.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.180
And it brings us to the following idea:

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.220
Yes, we do not know the parameters or

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.460
HiddenVector when we start
evaluating the biases of the coins.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.860
But let's start from
randomly chosen parameters.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.750
As soon as Parameters are chosen,
we can derive HiddenVector.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.800
As soon as HiddenVector is derived,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.490
we question our wisdom in selecting
the initial Parameters and

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:33.180
estimate Parameters using Data
and HiddenVector as I described before,

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:39.620
through the dot product. In this
way, we will arrive at the new parameters.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.850
As soon as the new Parameters are derived,

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:47.690
we once again want to evaluate
the HiddenVector and iterate.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.920
We came up with an approximation algorithm

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.990
for evaluating parameters for the coin flip,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.800
and let me show you how this algorithm works.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.410
We start from randomly selected Parameters.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:08.500
As soon as Parameters are selected,
we form HiddenVector,

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:14.770
so we assign each series of flips to
either the blue coin the or red coin.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.880
As soon as HiddenVector is derived,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.030
we can use this HiddenVector
to derive new parameters.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:26.860
As soon as these new parameters are derived,
we construct a new HiddenVector,

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.310
and we iterate until convergence.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.540
Does this algorithm remind you of something?

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.600
Of course! It is the k-means algorithm,

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.180
or something very similar to
the Lloyd algorithm in disguise.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:45.250
Where are Data, HiddenVector, and
parameters in k-means clustering?

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.489
Well, Data are obviously
the set of data points.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.820
What are Parameters?

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.710
Parameters of course
are the positions of the Centers.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.760
And what is HiddenVector?

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.220
HiddenVector is simply the assignment
of data points to k centers,

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:08.360
Which is an N-dimensional vector
with coordinates from 1 to k.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.610
This assignment HiddenVector is
shown below by the green coordinates.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:19.213
So, we figured out how to solve
the coin flipping problem and

00:04:19.213 --> 00:04:22.760
what relationship it has
to k-means clustering, and

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:28.253
now we'll talk about a more general
approach called "expectation maximization".

